# text
seg-bee5d056ef03 = Plain-language definitions of the terms used in Kshana. Each entry starts with a one-line "in plain terms" and then adds the precise meaning where it helps. Abbreviations not defined in a section are in the A-to-Z table near the end, <a1>Every other abbreviation, A to Z</a1>.

# text
seg-f8c5433d1026 = Navigation & timing

# text
seg-2a42d84608ea = <b>PNT — Positioning, Navigation, and Timing.</b> Knowing <em>where</em> you are, <em>which way</em> you are going, and <em>what time it is</em> — precisely. Modern PNT mostly comes from satellite signals (GNSS, the global navigation satellite systems defined next); Kshana studies what happens to <em>time</em> and <em>position</em> when those signals are lost.

# text
seg-6227db2c94e1 = <b>GNSS — Global Navigation Satellite System.</b> The satellite constellations that provide PNT: GPS (the Global Positioning System, USA (United States of America)), Galileo (EU), GLONASS (Russia's Global Navigation Satellite System), BeiDou (China). A receiver that can see ≥ { "4" } satellites can compute a full 3D position and time fix.

# text
seg-f8eda357e640 = <b>GNSS outage / denied / degraded / jammed.</b> When the satellite signals are unavailable (blocked, jammed, or out of view). During an outage the system must "coast" on its own onboard sensors. Kshana's whole purpose is to measure how well it coasts.

# text
seg-a3ade199f465 = <b>Holdover.</b> In plain terms: <em>how long the clock can keep good time on its own after it loses GNSS.</em> A better clock holds longer before its error crosses the allowed limit.

# text
seg-83b33c15312f = <b>Dead-reckoning.</b> In plain terms: <em>estimating where you are by adding up your measured motion, with no outside reference.</em> Errors accumulate, so a better inertial sensor drifts more slowly.

# text
seg-da9bcc994b9c = <b>Spec / threshold.</b> The maximum error you are allowed before the solution counts as "out of spec" — e.g. "timing must stay within { "20" } ns" or "position within { "100" } m".

# text
seg-6f37a60a30c7 = The sensors Kshana compares

# text
seg-308b59d89586 = <b>Clock (atomic clock).</b> A device that keeps time by counting an atom's natural oscillation. Two examples here:

# text
seg-3389d08be6bc = <b>CSAC — Chip-Scale Atomic Clock.</b> A small, deployed, commercial clock (the "classical" reference). Good, but drifts noticeably over a long outage.

# text
seg-3e121d8e6400 = <b>Optical lattice clock (e.g. strontium).</b> A far more stable "quantum" clock; the state of the art in laboratories, not yet flown in space.

# text
seg-4daa6cdf8ae2 = <b>Accelerometer.</b> Measures acceleration (change in motion). Integrated twice, it gives position — so any error grows quickly. <b>Cold-atom accelerometer</b> is the quantum version with much better long-term stability; <b>navigation-grade</b> is the classical one.

# text
seg-91793676c57c = <b>Gyroscope.</b> Measures rotation. A small rotation error tilts the platform, which leaks gravity into the horizontal direction and corrupts the position estimate.

# text
seg-2d36110effc3 = <b>IMU — Inertial Measurement Unit.</b> The accelerometer-and-gyroscope package itself: three axes of each, reporting specific force and angular rate. It measures motion; on its own it does not know where it is.

# text
seg-e3b6b40e0cd0 = <b>INS — Inertial Navigation System.</b> An IMU plus the "mechanization" that integrates its output into attitude, velocity and position. The INS is what dead-reckons through a GNSS outage, and its accuracy is set by the IMU error processes listed below.

# text
seg-a618ff7ee429 = <b>Time transfer.</b> Sending a precise time signal between two places (e.g. satellite to satellite). <b>Optical</b> links are far more precise than <b>RF</b> (radio-frequency) links.

# text
seg-847fbbeecfae = How errors are modelled (clock noise)

# text
seg-5cf197bdbbad = Clock error is built from standard "noise types", each with a known signature:

# text
# c1: q_wf
seg-3806d1b1cb50 = <b>White FM (white frequency modulation).</b> In plain terms: <em>fast, random jitter in the clock's rate.</em> Averaging it down improves with time. Parameter <c1/>.

# text
# c1: q_rw
seg-0793fdd3abab = <b>Random-walk FM.</b> In plain terms: <em>the clock's rate slowly wanders.</em> This dominates long outages. Parameter <c1/>.

# text
# c1: flicker_floor
seg-42b74c8b88ff = <b>Flicker FM (1/f noise).</b> In plain terms: <em>a stubborn noise floor the clock can never average below.</em> It is flat across averaging times. Parameter <c1/>.

# text
# c1: drift
seg-b3bd841a5a2d = <b>Aging / drift.</b> A slow, <em>predictable</em> change in rate over time; because it is predictable, Kshana's estimator removes it. Parameter <c1/>.

# text
# c1: q_va
seg-d929960492e4 = <b>VRW — Velocity Random Walk.</b> The accelerometer equivalent of white noise: random kicks to velocity that build up into position error. Parameter <c1/>.

# text
# c1: q_arw
seg-4a97f8d14174 = <b>ARW — Angular Random Walk.</b> The gyroscope equivalent: random kicks to orientation. Parameter <c1/>.

# text
seg-dcd03b326110 = How stability is measured

# text
# c1: σ_y(τ)
# c2: τ
# c3: σ_y(1 s) = 3×10⁻¹⁰
seg-2181832c6253 = <b>Allan deviation (<c1/>).</b> In plain terms: <em>the standard way to state how stable a clock is over a given averaging time <c2/>.</em> A clock datasheet quotes, e.g., <c3/>. Kshana validates its clock model by computing the Allan deviation of its own output and checking it matches the published number. (Reference: Riley, NIST SP { "1065" } — National Institute of Standards and Technology Special Publication { "1065" }.)

# text
seg-11cbe561e633 = <b>PSD — Power Spectral Density.</b> How a noise's power is distributed across frequencies; the formal way to specify white / random-walk / flicker noise.

# text
seg-0737d620eb5f = The figures of merit (how a run is scored)

# text
seg-20b66e0d4809 = The six operational PNT figures of merit Kshana reports (see the README "Output" table):

# text
seg-b17d08246e49 = <b>FoM — Figure of Merit.</b> One number that scores one aspect of a run, reported with its unit and whether it is validated or modelled. The six below are Kshana's operational FoMs.

# text
seg-b229a69fb4ac = <b>Positioning / Timing performance</b> — the size of the error, as RMS (root mean square, the quadratic average) and as the 95th percentile.

# text
seg-ba3ed5a28518 = <b>Autonomy</b> — the holdover duration (how long it stays in spec without GNSS).

# text
seg-13ecdd037098 = <b>Resilience</b> — how fast the error grows once GNSS is lost.

# text
seg-be0168f24d6e = <b>Availability</b> — the fraction of the run that has an in-spec solution.

# text
seg-44ef500b371d = <b>Integrity</b> — <em>can you trust the system's own estimate of its error?</em> Kshana reports the fraction of outage samples whose true error stays inside the filter's protective bound.

# text
seg-d1cc3819cde9 = <b>Security</b> — robustness to spoofing: a clock-stability-based spoof-detectability score (since { "v0.3.0" }), meaningful only when a spoofing-attack scenario is configured. It is <em>not</em> aviation-grade integrity: it computes no horizontal or vertical protection level (HPL/VPL) and runs no receiver autonomous integrity monitoring (RAIM), advanced or otherwise. Those come from the separate integrity and ARAIM (advanced RAIM) scenario kinds and are defined under "Integrity & augmentation" below. Export-sensitive.

# text
seg-5cae59204d9e = Terms used alongside the figures of merit:

# text
seg-7adc0e49a981 = <b>Spoofing.</b> In plain terms: <em>a fake satellite signal that lies about position or time.</em> A spoofer feeds the receiver a plausible but wrong answer, which is worse than jamming because the receiver may not notice.

# text
seg-2d2e6cbeade6 = <b>1-DOF — one degree of freedom.</b> A single axis. Kshana's scenario-pack position error is single-axis; a full 3-D answer needs the three-axis model.

# text
seg-14a32d281c4b = <b>Monte Carlo ensemble.</b> Running the same scenario many times with different random seeds and reporting the spread of the results rather than one run.

# text
seg-fcbea49a3592 = <b>CI — confidence interval.</b> The range a statistic is expected to fall in at a stated probability (for example { "95" } %). In the README's tables and badges "CI" more often means <b>continuous integration</b> — the automated build-and-test run on every change; the context says which.

# text
seg-310abfd6a64c = <b>k-sigma bound.</b> A bound set at <em>k</em> standard deviations of the filter's own uncertainty; the Integrity figure of merit counts how often the true error stays inside it.

# text
# c1: src/fom.rs
seg-ad26afbaa7b4 = <b>CEP / 2DRMS — circular error probable / twice the distance root mean square.</b> Two standard ways of stating 2-D horizontal accuracy: CEP is the radius holding { "50" } % of fixes; 2DRMS is twice the root-mean-square horizontal error. The library can compute both from a position covariance (<c1/>), but no scenario kind reports them yet.

# text
seg-fd93c1effba8 = Reading a result: the terms in the output

# text
seg-3432498093cf = These are the names you meet in a result file or the playground's summary, in plain terms first.

# text
# c1: holdover_s
seg-099bf2eb4602 = <b>Holdover (<c1/>).</b> In plain terms: <em>how many seconds the system stayed inside its error limit after GNSS was lost.</em> Kshana reports the shortest such coast across all outages in the run, measured on the run's time grid, so it is a lower bound at the time-step resolution. A holdover equal to the outage length means the limit was never crossed.

# text
# c1: timing_p95_ns
# c2: 0.0
seg-e68708a219f1 = <b>p95 (<c1/>).</b> In plain terms: <em>{ "95" } % of the time, the timing error was this small or smaller.</em> It is the 95th percentile of the error over the outage. A very small value can display as <c2/> in a short summary; the result JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) file holds the full-precision number.

# text
# c1: integrity
# c2: INTEGRITY.md
seg-4dfaee63262d = <b>Integrity (<c1/>).</b> In plain terms: <em>how often the system's own error estimate was honest.</em> It is the fraction of outage samples whose true error stayed inside the filter's k-sigma bound (defined below). A value near { "1" } means the estimator did not understate its error. It is not an aviation protection level; see "Integrity & augmentation" below and <a1><c2/></a1>.

# text
# c1: security
# c2: P_md
# c3: security n/a (no attack)
# c4: applicable: false
# c5: figure_tiers
seg-5af09f5129e0 = <b>Security score (<c1/>).</b> In plain terms: <em>how likely a spoofing attack is to be caught.</em> It is the probability that the configured attack is detected — one minus the missed-detection probability <c2/> — derived from the clock's stability. It only means something when the scenario configures a spoofing attack. With no attack configured, the one-line summary prints <c3/> and the result marks the figure <c4/> in its <c5/> block; the value stays in the document and means "not applicable", not "zero security".

# text
# c1: pdop_min
seg-70113d509997 = <b>PDOP — position dilution of precision (keys such as <c1/>).</b> In plain terms: <em>how much the satellite geometry magnifies ranging error into position error.</em> Position error is roughly PDOP times the ranging error, so lower is better. See <b>DOP</b> under "Estimation & geometry".

# text
# c1: σ_y(τ)
# c2: τ
# c3: sigma_y(1 s) = 3e-10
seg-a445bf9a3c5f = <b>sigma_y (<c1/>, the Allan deviation).</b> In plain terms: <em>how much a clock's rate wobbles when averaged over a time <c2/>.</em> A datasheet line such as <c3/> means the rate, averaged over one second, varies by about { "3" } parts in 10¹⁰. Smaller is a more stable clock. See "How stability is measured" above.

# text
# c1: σ_y²(τ) = q_wf / τ
# c2: q_wf = sigma_y(1 s)²
# c3: q_rw
# c4: drift
seg-03b4bb05148d = <b>q_wf — white-frequency noise intensity.</b> In plain terms: <em>the strength of the fast, random jitter in a clock's rate.</em> It is the coefficient in <c1/> for white frequency noise, so a scenario sets <c2/> from the datasheet's one-second Allan deviation. Its companions are <c3/> (random-walk frequency noise) and <c4/> (ageing); see "How errors are modelled" above.

# text
seg-074a1337ed99 = Telecom timing terms

# text
# c1: telecom-timing
# c2: TELECOM-TIMING.md
seg-9f61398628e4 = Telecom networks distribute time over packet networks (Precision Time Protocol) from a GNSS-fed reference clock, and the International Telecommunication Union Telecommunication Standardization Sector (ITU-T) sets limits on the time error each clock may add. These terms appear in the <c1/> kind; see <a1><c2/></a1>.

# text
seg-f48712300b30 = <b>TE — time error.</b> In plain terms: <em>how far a clock's time is from the reference time, at one instant.</em> Positive or negative, usually in nanoseconds.

# text
seg-ceda70656c6a = <b>max|TE| — maximum absolute time error.</b> In plain terms: <em>the worst time error seen over the measurement, ignoring its sign.</em> It is the headline limit most masks set.

# text
seg-38d2922cd91d = <b>cTE — constant time error.</b> In plain terms: <em>the steady offset part of the time error</em> — the average error that does not change over the measurement.

# text
seg-a4a61df332cd = <b>dTE — dynamic time error.</b> In plain terms: <em>the wobbling part of the time error</em> — what is left after the constant offset is removed. It is judged with MTIE and TDEV.

# text
seg-31c0a85788b1 = <b>MTIE — maximum time interval error.</b> In plain terms: <em>the largest swing in time error seen within any window of a given length.</em> For each window length it slides a window along the record and takes the biggest peak-to-peak change; a mask gives the largest allowed value per window length.

# text
seg-53fa12cab0c6 = <b>TDEV — time deviation.</b> In plain terms: <em>the typical (root-mean-square) wander of the time error at a given averaging time,</em> after short-term jitter is averaged out. It is a time-domain cousin of the Allan deviation and, like MTIE, is checked against a mask.

# text
seg-62b5eea14f6c = <b>PRTC — primary reference time clock.</b> The clock at the top of a telecom timing chain, normally steered by GNSS. Its performance limits are in ITU-T Recommendation G.8272.

# text
seg-8d6d4538981b = <b>ePRTC — enhanced primary reference time clock.</b> A PRTC with a tighter time-error limit, backed by a highly stable local atomic clock so it can hold time through a long GNSS loss. ITU-T Recommendation G.8272.1.

# text
seg-93fa4ac7c6fa = <b>T-BC — telecom boundary clock.</b> A network clock that receives time from upstream and passes it on downstream, adding a little time error of its own. ITU-T Recommendation G.8273.2.

# text
seg-52d32a5db6b8 = <b>T-TSC — telecom time slave clock.</b> The clock at the end of the chain that receives time and serves it to the equipment that needs it (for example a radio base station). Also covered by ITU-T Recommendation G.8273.2.

# text
seg-04d8ed12ca88 = <b>OCXO — oven-controlled crystal oscillator.</b> A quartz oscillator kept at a constant temperature inside a small oven, which makes it much more stable than an ordinary quartz oscillator. A common holdover clock in network equipment; it ages (drifts) noticeably over days.

# text
seg-6742f7ba35ac = <b>CSAC — chip-scale atomic clock.</b> A very small atomic clock (see "The sensors Kshana compares" above). More stable over long holdovers than an OCXO, at higher cost and power.

# text
seg-c0e0c18bd867 = Evidence tiers: VALIDATED, MODELLED, PARTNER

# text
# c1: VERIFICATION-MATRIX.md
seg-61c0aa107fd9 = Every capability in the machine-checked verification matrix (<a1><c1/></a1>) carries one of three tiers.

# text
seg-13fb20435df3 = <b>VALIDATED.</b> In plain terms: <em>checked against someone else's answer.</em> The capability's output was compared with an independent external oracle — real measured data, an independent library written by someone else, or published reference values — and agreed within a stated tolerance. A continuous-integration check refuses a VALIDATED row that names no external oracle.

# text
# c1: MODELLED-RATIONALE.md
seg-a5dfb48e4dcb = <b>MODELLED.</b> In plain terms: <em>built from sound physics and checked for internal consistency, but not yet compared with an outside answer.</em> The figures are meaningful for comparison and design trades; they are not evidence that a real device will perform that way. <a1><c1/></a1> explains why each such row is modelled.

# text
seg-0512b2d2145a = <b>PARTNER.</b> In plain terms: <em>this part belongs to a hardware or product-assurance partner, and Kshana claims nothing about it.</em> The row exists so the gap is visible rather than silent.

# text
seg-278dee6d3d4c = Spectrum and interference terms

# text
# c1: spectrum
# c2: SPECTRUM.md
seg-285a240c9958 = These terms appear in the <c1/> kind; see <a1><c2/></a1>.

# text
seg-db4fdef1c562 = <b>BPSK(n) — binary phase-shift keying at n × { "1.023" } Mchip/s.</b> The rectangular-chip modulation of GPS L1 coarse/acquisition (C/A) and L2 civil (L2C) (n = { "1" }) and GPS L5 and Galileo E5a (n = { "10" }). Its spectrum is a sinc² with nulls every n × { "1.023" } MHz.

# text
seg-cdd0f4194f42 = <b>BOC(m, n) — binary offset carrier.</b> A spreading code at n × { "1.023" } Mchip/s multiplied by a square-wave subcarrier at m × { "1.023" } MHz, which splits the spectrum into two lobes either side of the carrier.

# text
seg-313e27e29fbb = <b>MBOC({ "6,1" },1/11) — multiplexed binary offset carrier.</b> The power spectral density 10/11 × BOC({ "1,1" }) + 1/11 × BOC({ "6,1" }), agreed for Galileo E1 open service and GPS L1C; Galileo transmits it as composite BOC (CBOC).

# text
seg-7423f94e3018 = <b>CW — continuous wave.</b> An unmodulated carrier: a single tone.

# text
seg-55d03dd4af03 = <b>J/S — jammer-to-signal ratio.</b> Received jammer power over received signal power, in decibels.

# text
seg-b0f2974647d4 = <b>SSC — spectral separation coefficient.</b> κ = ∫ G_s(f) G_j(f) df over the receiver band: how much of a unit-power interferer's spectrum lands where the signal's does. The effective carrier-to-noise density is [1/(C/N₀) + (J/S)·κ]⁻¹.

# text
seg-c5affeee5269 = <b>Noise figure.</b> How much a receiver adds to the thermal noise, as a ratio F in decibels; the system noise temperature is T_ant + { "290" } K × (F − { "1" }).

# text
seg-5307c38d3036 = <b>Waterfall.</b> A time-frequency picture of a spectrum: frequency across, time down, colour for power.

# text
seg-3b1ac094fdc5 = <b>Welch estimate.</b> A power spectral density estimated by averaging windowed, overlapping periodograms (Welch { "1967" }).

# text
# c1: .sigmf-meta
# c2: .sigmf-data
# c3: cf32_le
# c4: ci16_le
# c5: ci8
seg-9901d64fb895 = <b>SigMF — Signal Metadata Format.</b> An open recording format: a JSON <c1/> file describing a raw <c2/> file of samples (here complex <c3/>, <c4/> or <c5/>).

# text
seg-83041671f422 = <b>IQ — in-phase and quadrature.</b> The two components of a complex baseband sample.

# text
seg-39e6ea8506b3 = LEO-PNT signal terms

# text
# c1: leo-signal
# c2: spectrum
# c3: LEO-SIGNAL.md
seg-36452ee3f5cc = These terms appear in the <c1/> kind and the multi-band <c2/> scenarios; see <a1><c3/></a1>.

# text
seg-8363a15f3c5a = <b>LEO-PNT — low Earth orbit positioning, navigation and timing.</b> Navigation signals broadcast from satellites a few hundred to about { "1200" } km up: stronger and faster-moving than GNSS from medium Earth orbit.

# text
seg-523469643156 = <b>RNSS, RDSS, MSS, EESS — Radio Navigation Satellite Service, Radio Determination Satellite Service, Mobile Satellite Service, Earth Exploration-Satellite Service.</b> ITU (International Telecommunication Union) service allocations a band can sit in.

# text
seg-d1e494623341 = <b>Pilot, data and acquisition components.</b> The parts of one signal: a data-free pilot for ranging, a data component that carries the navigation message, and a short, low-rate acquisition component that is easy to find.

# text
seg-cce232b7cd6a = <b>FDMA — frequency-division multiple access.</b> Components or satellites separated by carrier frequency rather than by code.

# text
seg-9d9476ee62b1 = <b>Gabor (RMS) bandwidth.</b> β = √(∫ f² G df / ∫ G df) over the receiver band: the spread of a signal's power in frequency, which sets how finely it can range.

# text
seg-6febe9c2e506 = <b>DLL — delay lock loop.</b> The code-tracking loop; its early-late correlator spacing and noise bandwidth set the thermal-noise ranging jitter.

# text
seg-5a55da97daf9 = <b>EFQPSK — enhanced Feher quadrature phase-shift keying.</b> A constant-envelope modulation (Xona Pulsar); approximated here by a rectangular-chip spectrum.

# text
seg-6dcbf1de9904 = <b>OFDM — orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing.</b> A multicarrier waveform (the Starlink downlink); drawn here as a flat band.

# text
seg-548b285c7c8e = <b>TEC — total electron content.</b> Electrons per square metre along the path; { "1" } TECU = 10¹⁶ el/m². The first-order ionospheric delay is { "40.3" }·TEC/f² metres.

# text
seg-dd7e0743f364 = <b>EIRP — effective isotropic radiated power.</b> Transmit power times antenna gain toward the receiver.

# text
seg-aba3b3eaba85 = Integrity & augmentation

# text
seg-eab1b2c6728e = Integrity is the <em>trust</em> question: not "how big is my error?" but "can I bound it, and will I be told when the bound is broken?" These are the aviation terms for that.

# text
# c1: src/raim.rs
seg-696a73449c49 = <b>RAIM — Receiver Autonomous Integrity Monitoring.</b> In plain terms: <em>the receiver checks the satellites against each other.</em> With more satellites than the four a fix needs, the leftovers (the residuals) should be small; a large residual means one satellite is lying, and the receiver can say so without help from the ground. Implemented in <a1><c1/></a1>.

# text
# c1: ARAIM_REFERENCE.md
seg-89237433f01a = <b>ARAIM — Advanced Receiver Autonomous Integrity Monitoring.</b> The modern, dual-frequency multi-constellation form of RAIM. It is fed an integrity support message stating each satellite's assumed error and fault probability, and returns protection levels rather than a pass/fail flag. Full treatment, with every assumption, in <a1><c1/></a1>.

# text
# c1: leo-navmsg
seg-4cc13de05193 = <b>SISRE — Signal-in-Space Range Error.</b> In plain terms: <em>how far off a satellite's broadcast orbit and clock make the range a typical user measures.</em> The orbit error is split into radial, along-track and cross-track parts and weighted by how much each shows up along an average line of sight, then combined with the clock error (Montenbruck et al. { "2018" }). For a low Earth orbit satellite the along- and cross-track parts weigh far more than for GPS, because users see it from far off nadir. Used by the <c1/> kind.

# text
# c1: leo-navmsg
# c2: kepler-rac
seg-847e40a033e1 = <b>RAC — Radial, Along-track, Cross-track.</b> The three directions an orbit error is resolved into: out from the Earth's centre, along the direction of flight, and perpendicular to the orbit plane. The <c1/> kind's <c2/> model adds a correction polynomial in each.

# text
seg-b725d23c839e = <b>SVID — Space-Vehicle Identifier.</b> The number a navigation message uses to name the satellite that sent it.

# text
seg-b0f850eb04b1 = <b>IOD — Issue of Data.</b> A counter that changes whenever a satellite's navigation message content changes, so a receiver can tell a new message from a repeat. (Not to be confused with an in-orbit demonstration, also abbreviated IOD, as in ESA's Celeste IOD.)

# text
seg-985a5e9c9ce9 = <b>TOW — Time of Week.</b> Seconds since the start of the current GNSS week (Sunday { "00" }:{ "00" }).

# text
# c1: 0x1864CFB
seg-1fc62dca46d6 = <b>CRC-24Q — 24-bit cyclic redundancy check (Qualcomm polynomial).</b> The error-detecting check RTCM { "10403" } and Galileo messages carry: a 24-bit remainder of the message bits divided by the polynomial <c1/>. A receiver recomputes it and discards a message that does not match.

# text
# c1: sigma_ure_m
# c2: src/raim.rs
seg-abb4679dab23 = <b>URE — User Range Error.</b> In plain terms: <em>how wrong a satellite's own broadcast orbit and clock make every range to it.</em> Also called the signal-in-space error (SISE); it is the per-satellite error budget ARAIM and RAIM are fed (<c1/> in <a1><c2/></a1>).

# text
seg-158c210fd3fe = <b>MHSS — Multiple Hypothesis Solution Separation.</b> The method ARAIM uses. For each way the constellation could be faulted it forms a solution that excludes the suspect satellites, and bounds how far that sub-solution can sit from the all-in-view one.

# text
seg-671711003a6f = <b>HPL / VPL — Horizontal / Vertical Protection Level.</b> In plain terms: <em>the radius the system promises the true error is inside, at a stated integrity risk.</em> It is a bound the algorithm computes, not a measured error. A run is available when the protection level stays under the alert limit the operation allows (VAL, the vertical alert limit; HAL, the horizontal one).

# text
# c1: src/sbas.rs
seg-5bbd2d664862 = <b>SBAS — Satellite-Based Augmentation System.</b> A ground network that watches GNSS, computes corrections and integrity bounds, and broadcasts them from geostationary satellites. <b>WAAS — Wide Area Augmentation System</b> is the United States one; EGNOS (the European Geostationary Navigation Overlay Service) is the European equivalent. Kshana computes protection levels in the DO-229E weighted-least-squares form (DO-229E is the RTCA minimum operational performance standard for SBAS receivers; RTCA was formerly the Radio Technical Commission for Aeronautics) (<a1><c1/></a1>).

# text
seg-ba8b448582af = Estimation & geometry

# text
seg-2eb6e72496b8 = <b>Estimator.</b> The algorithm that predicts the true position/time during an outage from the sensor model. Kshana has an analytic holdover predictor and a <b>Kalman filter</b>.

# text
seg-9b4aa612c5a9 = <b>Kalman filter.</b> In plain terms: <em>an algorithm that tracks a quantity and also tracks how uncertain it is.</em> Kshana uses its uncertainty bound to compute Integrity.

# text
seg-8fda81dfccc9 = <b>EKF — Extended Kalman Filter.</b> A Kalman filter for a non-linear problem: it linearises the model about the current estimate at every step. The standard choice for coupling GNSS to an INS.

# text
seg-9bc2c6f74b29 = <b>UKF — Unscented Kalman Filter.</b> The same job without linearising: it pushes a small set of deterministically chosen sample points through the true non-linear model and rebuilds the mean and covariance from where they land. Better behaved when the non-linearity is strong, which is why the orbit determination uses it.

# text
seg-a1e52820e3a0 = <b>DOP — Dilution Of Precision.</b> In plain terms: <em>how much the satellites' geometry amplifies ranging error into position or time error.</em> Position error ≈ PDOP × ranging error, so a low number is good geometry. The family: <b>GDOP</b> (geometric — position and time together), <b>PDOP</b> (position), <b>HDOP</b> (horizontal), <b>VDOP</b> (vertical), <b>TDOP</b> (time).

# text
seg-e769a91a3e4b = <b>Walker constellation.</b> A standard way to describe a satellite constellation by its number of orbital planes and satellites per plane (GPS is roughly a 24/6 Walker shell).

# text
seg-b557933a4e4d = <b>Elevation mask.</b> The minimum angle above the local horizon at which a satellite is considered usable; signals too low are excluded.

# text
seg-3ac8e11c3e33 = <b>Occultation.</b> When the Earth physically blocks the line of sight to a satellite.

# text
# c1: max(elevation mask, skyline)
# c2: SKYLINE.md
seg-249bf472cb00 = <b>Skyline (skyline mask).</b> In plain terms: <em>the outline of buildings, cranes or hills against the sky, as an elevation for every azimuth.</em> A satellite counts as in view only above <c1/> at its azimuth, and one above the mask but behind the skyline is "masked by the skyline". Kshana builds one from a table, a street canyon, sectors and terrain (their pointwise maximum) or a REPRESENTATIVE preset; line of sight only, no reflection or diffraction (<a1><c2/></a1>).

# text
# c1: SKYLINE.md
seg-620a88820a2d = <b>Sky geometry.</b> Which satellites a site sees under a skyline and what that does to the solution: the DOP factors, the east-north-up covariance of the least-squares fix and its <b>error ellipse</b>, the horizontal region that holds the stated share of the fixes ({ "95" } % by default) for independent, equal Gaussian range errors. MODELLED geometry under a stated error model, not a receiver's real error (<a1><c1/></a1>).

# text
# c1: src/integrity/mod.rs
seg-f0e610122425 = <b>CTI — Composed Timing Integrity.</b> Protection levels for <em>time</em> rather than position, computed across a hand-over between different time sources — for example from a satellite-checked (RAIM-available) clock to a free-running one in holdover — so the bound stays valid through the transition (<a1><c1/></a1>).

# text
# c1: src/benchmark/mod.rs
seg-56642f614c3f = <b>TIB — Timing Integrity Benchmark.</b> A yardstick for <em>other people's</em> timing monitors: it injects a menu of faults and checks whether the monitor's stated protection level actually bounded its error. It makes no accuracy claim of its own, so it cannot overstate one (<a1><c1/></a1>).

# text
seg-8b89f8b913b9 = Orbits & cislunar

# text
# c1: src/cr3bp.rs
seg-ba6b669eb8ba = <b>CR3BP — Circular Restricted Three-Body Problem.</b> The Earth–Moon system treated as two bodies on circular orbits about their common centre of mass, plus a spacecraft too light to pull back. Near the Moon, a two-body Kepler ellipse is simply the wrong shape; the CR3BP is the cheapest dynamics that is right (<a1><c1/></a1>).

# text
seg-2726a22bf14c = <b>NRHO — Near-Rectilinear Halo Orbit.</b> A periodic CR3BP orbit that swings close over one lunar pole and far out over the other, so it is almost a straight line end-on — hence "near-rectilinear". It is the orbit chosen for the lunar Gateway, and it keeps near-continuous line of sight to Earth.

# text
seg-2b2e6d04376c = <b>DRO — Distant Retrograde Orbit.</b> Another CR3BP family: a large, stable orbit that circles the Moon backwards as seen in the rotating frame.

# text
# c1: finals2000A
# c2: --eop
seg-6bb9229aee5c = <b>EOP — Earth Orientation Parameters.</b> The measured, slightly irregular wobble and spin of the Earth (polar motion, UT1 (Universal Time { "1" }, Earth-rotation time)−UTC — Earth-rotation time minus Coordinated Universal Time — and length of day) that is needed to turn an Earth-fixed position into an inertial one to better than metres. The IERS (International Earth Rotation and Reference Systems Service) publishes them; Kshana reads its <c1/> file via <c2/>.

